# Default words for routine moves in the Caro-Kann lines, written for Black; a line's own note replaces these.
w O-O: White castles.
w Nf3: White develops and covers e5 and d4.
w d4: White takes the centre.
w c3: White supports d4.
w Nc3: White develops and adds pressure on d5.
w Bd3: White develops, aiming at h7, and offers to trade your light bishop.
w Be2: White develops modestly and prepares to castle.
w Qxd3: White recaptures, developing the queen.
w exd5: White releases the centre: expect ...cxd5 next.
w c4: White attacks d5 from the side, aiming for an isolated pawn or a Panov.
w Bb5: White pins or pressures the c6-knight.
w Bb5+: White develops with check.
w h3: White asks the bishop on g4 to decide.
w Be3: White backs up d4 and keeps c5 in view.
w e5: White gains space and blocks your f6-knight's best route.
w f3: White supports e4, offering a gambit-style centre.
w Bc4: White develops actively, looking at f7.
w cxd5: White trades on d5 to open lines.
w Bg5: White pins the f6-knight.
w d3: White plays a modest set-up and keeps e4 protected.
w Bb3: White retreats the bishop but keeps it aimed at d5.
w Nbd2: White develops, backing up e4 or f3.
w Bf4: White activates the dark bishop before e3.
w cxd4: White recaptures, keeping a pawn on d4.
w Ne2: White develops, with an eye on f4 or g3.
w Qxf3: White recaptures with the queen.
w h4: White gains kingside space, hunting your bishop.
w Bd2: White blocks the check or develops quietly.
w Bxd7+: White trades bishops with check.
w Nge2: White develops the knight towards f4 or g3.
w f4: White takes more space and backs e5.
w Bxc4: White recaptures with the bishop.
w Ng5: White jumps forward, eyeing f7 and h7.
w Ne5: White plants the knight on the central outpost.
w Qf3: An early queen sortie that hits f7 and looks at f5.
w Nxf3: White recaptures with the knight, keeping open lines.
w Qe2: White connects the rooks and adds to the e-file.
w Bxf3: White recaptures with the bishop.
w Nxe4: White recaptures on e4.
w Qd3: White develops the queen and watches the b1-h7 diagonal.
w Ng3: White retreats the knight, gaining a move on your bishop.
w Nd2: White develops, supporting e4.
w O-O-O: White castles long, signalling a kingside attack.
w Ngf3: White develops the last knight.
w Qxe4: White recaptures with the queen, which is well placed.
w g3: A King's Indian Attack set-up: the bishop heads for g2.
w Nxf6+: White trades knights with check.
w dxe4: White recaptures, leaving a symmetrical structure.
w Kxd1: White recaptures with the king; the queens are gone.
w fxe4: White recaptures, taking a broad centre.
w Qe1: The queen frees d1 and heads for the kingside or a5.
w a3: White takes b4 from your pieces.
w Bxd6: White trades the dark bishops.
w dxe5: White takes on e5.
w g4: White hunts your bishop, with kingside space as a bonus.
w dxc5: White takes on c5, aiming to win time on your bishop.
w Qa4+: White checks along the diagonal.
w Bxc6+: White takes the knight with check, damaging your pawn structure.
w N5f3: The knight goes back after h6 has chased it.
w Nxe5: White recaptures on e5.
w Nc5: The knight jumps forward to attack b7.
w Nb3: The knight retreats, with an eye on c5 and a5.
w N2g3: The other knight joins, heading for your bishop.
w Qxd5: White trades queens on d5.
w Nxg6: White takes the bishop, doubling your pawns but opening the h-file for you.
w Nxd4: White recaptures on d4.
w Nh3: The knight goes to h3 because f3 is covered.
w Qe3: White defends the d-pawn and develops.
w Qh5: A direct try against f7 that usually wastes time.
w Nf2: The knight retreats to guard the e4 square.
w Bg2: The fianchetto bishop lands on the long diagonal.
w Re1: White takes the e-file.
w Qa4: White pins the knight on d7.
w exd6: White takes the pawn on d6.
w Bxd3: White recaptures.
w Bxf7+: This is the point: the f7 pawn was only guarded by the king.
w Qb3: The queen hits both the bishop and b7.
w Qxb4+: The queen wins the bishop with check.
w Qc4+: Another check, and the c2 pawn falls next.
w Qxc2: White collects the last loose pawn: White is winning.
w Bd2: White blocks the check or develops quietly.
b e6: Support d5 and open the f8-bishop.
b Nf6: Develop and hit e4.
b Nc6: Develop and add pressure on d4.
b c5: Strike at d4: this is the Caro-Kann's main freeing break.
b Be7: A modest, solid square. Castle next.
b O-O: Castle: your king is safe and the rooks connect.
b Bg4: Develop outside the chain with a pin on the f3-knight.
b Nd7: Develop the queen's knight, ready to support e5 or challenge on f6.
b Qb6: Hit b2 and d4: the queen uses the dark-square gap that your bishop left behind.
b Bd6: Develop to the best diagonal, offering to trade dark bishops.
b cxd5: Recapture with the c-pawn: the classic Caro structure with the c-file open.
b Ngf6: Develop the second knight, challenging e4.
b Bxd3: Trade off White's attacking bishop.
b dxe4: Trade on e4 and take your share of the centre.
b Bxf3: Give the bishop for the knight to remove a key defender of d4 and e5.
b Bf5: Develop the bishop outside the chain, the whole point of the Caro-Kann move order.
b Ne7: Develop towards f5 or g6, leaving the f-pawn free.
b Nbd7: Develop the queen's knight.
b e5: Break in the centre, which gives your bishops free play.
b cxd4: Open the c-file for your rook and release the tension.
b d5: Take the centre: this is the Caro-Kann's plan after ...c6.
b h6: Give the bishop a square, or stop Ng5.
b dxc4: Take on c4, making White spend a tempo with the bishop.
b g6: Prepare a fianchetto, giving the dark bishop a long diagonal.
b Nb6: Retreat the knight to a square where it hits c4 or d5.
b Be6: Develop the bishop to shield d5.
b Nxd5: Recapture in the centre.
b Nxe5: Recapture on e5, centralising the knight.
b Bd7: Block the check and develop.
b Bg6: Step back, keeping the bishop on its diagonal.
b Rc8: Take the c-file so c5 has more force.
b exf3: Take the gambit pawn. You can give it back later for a good position.
b Bb4: Pin the knight and develop quickly.
b Qxd7: Recapture with the queen and connect the rooks.
b Nge7: Develop the knight towards f5 or g6.
b a6: Stop Nb5 and Bb5, and keep your queenside secure.
b b6: Prepare ...Bb7 or back up the c5 square.
b Nxf6: Recapture on f6, developing a knight back into play.
b Qxd1+: Trade queens: the ending is roughly level, and White has lost castling.
b Bg7: The bishop arrives on the long diagonal, aimed at d4.
b exd4: Take on d4.
b Ba5: Keep the bishop aimed at the White king.
b b5: Gain queenside space and hit the bishop.
b Qxd6: Recapture with the queen.
b exd5: Recapture, opening the e-file for your rook.
b h5: Stop the g-pawn and h-pawn from marching.
b g5: Take kingside space and stop f4 plans.
b Qxc6: Recapture with the queen, keeping the structure intact.
b Qd5: Centralise the queen and offer a trade that eases your defence.
b Bxe4: Trade your bishop for White's active knight.
b Qxd4: Take the d4 pawn: if it is free, take it.
b Qh4+: Check, disturbing White's king position.
b Qf6: Step away from danger, keeping the extra pawn safe.
b hxg6: Recapture towards the centre and open the h-file.
b Bc5: Develop actively, with pressure on d4 and f2.
b Nfd7: Retreat the knight to prepare ...c5.
b Nxd7: Recapture on d7.
b Nf5: Land on the outpost, attacking d4.
b Nxe4: Trade the knights on e4.
b Bh6: The bishop goes to its active diagonal.
b Re8: The rook supports ...e5 and takes the e-file.
b Rb8: Save the rook from the bishop's attack.
b d6: A compact reply.
b exd6: Recapture towards the centre.
b exd3: Take the pawn. Now your structure stays compact.
b Qa5+: Check, gaining a move and developing the queen.
b Qc7: Guard h7 and e5 by centralising the queen.
b Nh6: Develop the knight towards f5.
b Bh5: Keep the pin.
b Bb4+: A natural-looking check. It turns out to be the wrong moment.
b dxc3: The greedy capture. It runs into a sting on f7.
b Ke7: The king must step up.
b c2+: Discovered check trying to promote; it is the last trick.
b Kxf7: Take the bishop; the king stays exposed.
b Ke8: The king steps back, but c2 falls and White is simply better.
b d4: Gain space and hit the knight.
w Bf3: White puts the bishop on the long diagonal, aimed at d5 and b7.
w Qd4: White centralises the queen and hits g7.
w Qe2+: White checks on the open e-file.
w Qxd4: White recaptures with the queen.
w d5: White pushes past your knight on c6, gaining space with tempo.
w dxc6: White takes the c-pawn, offering a gambit for development.
w gxf3: White recaptures with the g-pawn, opening the g-file.
w h5: White gains more kingside space.
b Nbxd7: Recapture with the knight, which heads for f6 or b6.
b Nd3+: Check that forces White to give up the f1-bishop.
b Ne5: Centralise the knight: it heads for d3.
b Nxc6: Recapture, developing the knight in return for the pawn.
b bxc6: Recapture towards the centre, doubling nothing and keeping d5 supported.
b cxd3: Recapture: you keep an extra pawn on d3, at least for now.
b exf6: Recapture with the e-pawn: your structure is healthy, and pieces develop fast. White has a queenside majority.
b gxf6: Recapture with the g-pawn: active piece play and an open g-file in return for a split structure.
w Qc2: White lines the queen up with the bishop against h7.
b e4: Gain space and hit the f3-knight.
